Relevant columns and their potential usage
• collisions.case_id – join‐key that lets us attach each crash to its calendar year  
• case_ids.db_year – the year in which the crash was recorded  
• collisions.pcf_violation_category – text description of the traffic‑law violation judged to have caused the crash (i.e., the “cause”)  
• collisions.primary_collision_factor – a parallel wording of the same idea (useful if pcf_violation_category is blank)  

Typical conditions that will be useful  
• WHERE co."pcf_violation_category" IS NOT NULL        -- ignore rows with no cause recorded  
• JOIN "collisions" AS co ON co."case_id" = ci."case_id"  -- attach the year  
• GROUP BY ci."db_year", co."pcf_violation_category"      -- count causes per year  

Below are ten exploratory SQL statements, from very simple to the one that answers  
“In which year were the two most common causes of traffic accidents different from those in other years?”  
All statements are capped with LIMIT 20 just to keep the output readable while you explore.  

```sql
-- 1. List every year that exists in the data set
SELECT DISTINCT ci."db_year"
FROM "case_ids" AS ci
ORDER BY ci."db_year"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. See how the “cause” field actually looks
SELECT DISTINCT co."pcf_violation_category"
FROM "collisions" AS co
WHERE co."pcf_violation_category" IS NOT NULL
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Count the overall frequency of each cause (all years combined)
SELECT co."pcf_violation_category",
       COUNT(*) AS crash_cnt
FROM   "collisions" AS co
WHERE  co."pcf_violation_category" IS NOT NULL
GROUP  BY co."pcf_violation_category"
ORDER  BY crash_cnt DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Same as #3 but tied to the calendar year
SELECT ci."db_year",
       co."pcf_violation_category",
       COUNT(*) AS crash_cnt
FROM   "collisions" AS co
JOIN   "case_ids"  AS ci ON ci."case_id" = co."case_id"
WHERE  co."pcf_violation_category" IS NOT NULL
GROUP  BY ci."db_year", co."pcf_violation_category"
ORDER  BY ci."db_year", crash_cnt DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Top‑2 causes in 2020
SELECT co."pcf_violation_category",
       COUNT(*) AS crash_cnt
FROM   "collisions" AS co
JOIN   "case_ids"  AS ci ON ci."case_id" = co."case_id"
WHERE  ci."db_year" = 2020
  AND  co."pcf_violation_category" IS NOT NULL
GROUP  BY co."pcf_violation_category"
ORDER  BY crash_cnt DESC
LIMIT 2;
```

```sql
-- 6. Top‑2 causes in 2019
SELECT co."pcf_violation_category",
       COUNT(*) AS crash_cnt
FROM   "collisions" AS co
JOIN   "case_ids"  AS ci ON ci."case_id" = co."case_id"
WHERE  ci."db_year" = 2019
  AND  co."pcf_violation_category" IS NOT NULL
GROUP  BY co."pcf_violation_category"
ORDER  BY crash_cnt DESC
LIMIT 2;
```

```sql
-- 7. Top‑2 causes for every year (window function ranks rows inside each year)
WITH ranked AS (
  SELECT ci."db_year",
         co."pcf_violation_category",
         COUNT(*)                     AS crash_cnt,
         ROW_NUMBER() OVER (
             PARTITION BY ci."db_year"
             ORDER BY COUNT(*) DESC
         ) AS rn
  FROM   "collisions" AS co
  JOIN   "case_ids"  AS ci ON ci."case_id" = co."case_id"
  WHERE  co."pcf_violation_category" IS NOT NULL
  GROUP  BY ci."db_year", co."pcf_violation_category"
)
SELECT *
FROM   ranked
WHERE  rn <= 2
ORDER  BY "db_year", rn
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Find the two most common causes across *all* years (the global Top‑2)
SELECT co."pcf_violation_category",
       COUNT(*) AS crash_cnt
FROM   "collisions" AS co
JOIN   "case_ids"  AS ci ON ci."case_id" = co."case_id"
WHERE  co."pcf_violation_category" IS NOT NULL
GROUP  BY co."pcf_violation_category"
ORDER  BY crash_cnt DESC
LIMIT 2;
```

```sql
-- 9. For each year, assemble its Top‑2 causes into one comma‑delimited string
WITH ranked AS (
  SELECT ci."db_year",
         co."pcf_violation_category",
         COUNT(*)                     AS crash_cnt,
         ROW_NUMBER() OVER (
             PARTITION BY ci."db_year"
             ORDER BY COUNT(*) DESC
         ) AS rn
  FROM   "collisions" AS co
  JOIN   "case_ids"  AS ci ON ci."case_id" = co."case_id"
  WHERE  co."pcf_violation_category" IS NOT NULL
  GROUP  BY ci."db_year", co."pcf_violation_category"
),
top2 AS (
  SELECT "db_year",
         GROUP_CONCAT("pcf_violation_category", ', ') AS top2_causes
  FROM   ranked
  WHERE  rn <= 2
  GROUP  BY "db_year"
)
SELECT *
FROM   top2
ORDER  BY "db_year"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. FINAL ANSWER:
-- Which year’s Top‑2 causes are NOT the same as the global Top‑2 set?
WITH global_top AS (                             -- the two most frequent overall
  SELECT co."pcf_violation_category"
  FROM   "collisions" AS co
  JOIN   "case_ids"  AS ci ON ci."case_id" = co."case_id"
  WHERE  co."pcf_violation_category" IS NOT NULL
  GROUP  BY co."pcf_violation_category"
  ORDER  BY COUNT(*) DESC
  LIMIT 2
),
yearly_ranked AS (                               -- rank causes inside each year
  SELECT ci."db_year",
         co."pcf_violation_category",
         COUNT(*)                       AS crash_cnt,
         ROW_NUMBER() OVER (
             PARTITION BY ci."db_year"
             ORDER BY COUNT(*) DESC
         ) AS rn
  FROM   "collisions" AS co
  JOIN   "case_ids"  AS ci ON ci."case_id" = co."case_id"
  WHERE  co."pcf_violation_category" IS NOT NULL
  GROUP  BY ci."db_year", co."pcf_violation_category"
),
yearly_top2 AS (                               -- keep only rank 1 and 2 of every year
  SELECT "db_year", "pcf_violation_category"
  FROM   yearly_ranked
  WHERE  rn <= 2
),
diff_years AS (                                -- years whose set <> global set
  SELECT yt."db_year"
  FROM   yearly_top2 AS yt
  GROUP  BY yt."db_year"
  HAVING SUM( CASE
                WHEN yt."pcf_violation_category" IN (SELECT * FROM global_top)
                THEN 1 ELSE 0
              END
            ) < 2                            -- missing at least one of the global Top‑2
)
SELECT DISTINCT "db_year"
FROM   diff_years
ORDER  BY "db_year"
LIMIT 20;
```

